# M9 — the production role guard's CREATEROLE predicate made unmatchable.
#
# WHY THIS PREDICATE EXISTS. The production guard (POSTURE_SQL in pool.ts) and the harness
# guard (assertTestConnectionIdentity) are supposed to state one rule, and they had
# DRIFTED: the harness tested rolcreaterole, rolcreatedb and membership of
# pg_read_all_data / pg_write_all_data, and production tested NONE of them. The harness
# holding the stricter rule is the worst direction for that drift, because the suite's job
# is to notice a weakness on the production side — a role that had acquired CREATEROLE was
# refused by the tests and ACCEPTED by the application.
#
# WHY THE CATCHER ASSERTS A REASON CODE AND NOT JUST A REJECTION. Pointing a pool at
# statesman_migrator ALREADY fails, and did before this predicate existed: the migrator is
# a member of statesman_owner, which owns every public relation, and ownership bypasses RLS
# (V4). So "a PrivilegeAssertionError was thrown" is worth nothing here — it stays true
# with this predicate deleted. The named test asserts the guard reports CREATEROLE
# specifically, which is the only thing this mutation can take away.
id: M9
title: privileged-role's CREATEROLE reason is ANDed with false, so no role ever matches it
expect: caught
proves: the production role guard's CREATEROLE predicate has a control that names the reason it reported
expect-test: CREATEROLE: refuses a connection that can SET ROLE to a role holding CREATEROLE

file: src/server/db/privileged-role.ts
count: 1
@@FIND
    sql: (a) => `${a}.rolcreaterole`,
@@REPLACE
    sql: (a) => `${a}.rolcreaterole and false`,
@@END
